WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.448
When a caller waits in a queue, the platform decides which agent gets the call.

00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:10.101
This video explains that chain, one link at a time:

00:00:10.101 --> 00:00:15.869
who may take a call, skills, capacity, the routing strategy, sticky agents, priority

00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:18.881
and aging, declined offers, and overflow.

00:00:18.881 --> 00:00:23.367
Calls never connect on this demo site, so what happens in a live

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.290
call is shown in slides.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:35.195
When work is waiting in a queue, the platform looks for an agent in four steps.

00:00:35.195 --> 00:00:37.692
First, who may take a call at all.

00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:42.248
An agent must be Available, entitled to the queue, signed in to it,

00:00:42.248 --> 00:00:44.120
and have a live session.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.579
Second, two filters.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.062
Skills remove agents who lack a required skill.

00:00:50.062 --> 00:00:53.414
Capacity removes agents who are already full.

00:00:53.414 --> 00:00:58.935
Third, the queue's routing strategy ranks whoever is left, and the best ranked

00:00:58.935 --> 00:01:00.710
agent gets the offer.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.749
Fourth, history and bonuses.

00:01:03.749 --> 00:01:06.509
A sticky agent is moved to the top.

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:11.221
An agent who already declined or missed this call is moved back.

00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:14.250
This order is the same for every queue.

00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:20.631
Open Queues and add a queue.

00:01:20.631 --> 00:01:23.991
On Routing, Routing strategy has three choices.

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:29.000
Longest idle offers the call to the agent who has been available longest.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.502
Round robin offers it to the agent who finished work longest ago.

00:01:33.502 --> 00:01:38.701
Least busy offers it to the agent with the fewest active interactions, which

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:42.695
matters little when agents take one voice call at a time.

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:47.267
Prefer the last assigned agent turns on the sticky bonus.

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:52.810
It applies to work that returns to the queue with its last owner still recorded.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.579
A new inbound call has no previous agent, so a returning customer is

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:59.771
not sent to the same person.

00:01:59.771 --> 00:02:04.347
Sticky never overrides skills, capacity or the order of declines.

00:02:04.803 --> 00:02:08.300
Reservation timeout is how long an offer rings.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.710
Unanswered offer action decides what happens when the agent does not answer:

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.943
Requeue, Send to voicemail, or Reject call.

00:02:21.164 --> 00:02:25.002
The Skills card has Required skills and Skill requirements.

00:02:25.002 --> 00:02:30.022
A requirement names a Skill, a Minimum proficiency, whether it is Required, and

00:02:30.022 --> 00:02:31.793
Relax after, in seconds.

00:02:31.793 --> 00:02:34.746
A required skill removes agents who lack it.

00:02:34.746 --> 00:02:39.470
Relax after drops the requirement once the caller has waited that long, so

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.304
a rare skill cannot hold a caller forever.

00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:46.340
A requirement that is not required removes nobody.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.378
It ranks agents who have the skill higher.

00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:55.517
The agent's own skills, with a proficiency from one to five, are set on Agent entitlements.

00:02:55.974 --> 00:03:01.578
Agent entitlements also has Queue preferences, with a Priority and a Delay in seconds.

00:03:01.578 --> 00:03:06.024
They matter when an agent becomes free and picks the next work, not

00:03:06.024 --> 00:03:09.314
when a call arrives and several agents are idle.

00:03:10.914 --> 00:03:15.135
Capacity is how many interactions an agent can hold at once.

00:03:15.135 --> 00:03:19.036
It is one by default, and no screen has a field for it:

00:03:19.036 --> 00:03:21.914
it comes from a recipe or a deployment.

00:03:21.914 --> 00:03:26.136
An agent on a voice call is never offered another call, so a

00:03:26.136 --> 00:03:29.014
higher capacity only matters for texts.

00:03:33.201 --> 00:03:36.301
A caller's priority decides who is served first.

00:03:36.301 --> 00:03:40.780
For a call that comes through an entry point, the entry point's Priority

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.856
is where it starts, and it replaces the queue's Default priority.

00:03:44.856 --> 00:03:50.311
The queue's Default priority applies to callbacks, and to lines that have no entry point.

00:03:51.911 --> 00:03:53.884
Two raises are automatic.

00:03:53.884 --> 00:03:59.359
A caller who rings again within four hours of their last call is raised to High.

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:05.279
A customer who returns a callback you scheduled, within 24 hours, is raised to Highest.

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:07.507
Priority is only ever raised.

00:04:07.507 --> 00:04:11.581
These raises apply to calls that go straight to the queue.

00:04:11.581 --> 00:04:16.164
A call that first plays a welcome message, a recording notice or a

00:04:16.164 --> 00:04:19.411
menu is queued at the entry point's priority.

00:04:21.008 --> 00:04:23.049
On Service levels, the S.L.A.

00:04:23.049 --> 00:04:26.140
threshold and Age waiting items past the S.L.A.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.657
threshold add aging.

00:04:27.657 --> 00:04:31.739
An item moves up one priority level once it has waited twice the

00:04:31.739 --> 00:04:35.472
threshold, then one more level for each further threshold.

00:04:35.472 --> 00:04:39.438
The wait counts from the first time the caller joined a queue.

00:04:43.638 --> 00:04:47.681
When an agent declines an offer, or lets it ring out, the call

00:04:47.681 --> 00:04:49.406
goes back to the queue.

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:54.698
Both are recorded, and the agent returns to Available at the back of the idle line.

00:04:55.448 --> 00:04:59.276
The next offer goes first to an agent who has not had this

00:04:59.276 --> 00:05:03.523
call, then to agents who declined it earlier, the earliest first.

00:05:03.523 --> 00:05:08.188
If one agent is the only one free, that agent is offered the call again.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:18.255
On Hours and overflow, Overflow queue and Overflow after set the first hop.

00:05:18.255 --> 00:05:22.601
Overflow chain adds more hops, each with its own wait.

00:05:22.601 --> 00:05:25.570
The wait starts again at every hop.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:30.107
A caller is never sent back to a queue they have already visited.

00:05:30.107 --> 00:05:33.901
The chain that applies is the one on the queue the caller is

00:05:33.901 --> 00:05:37.063
in now, so a path from A through B to C needs B's

00:05:37.063 --> 00:05:38.558
own chain to name C.

00:05:38.558 --> 00:05:42.928
On Limits, Maximum wait and its action decide what happens to a caller

00:05:42.928 --> 00:05:44.365
who waits too long.

00:05:48.551 --> 00:05:53.575
When a call does not reach the agent you expect, open Reports, Queue

00:05:53.575 --> 00:05:57.105
and Routing, Agent availability and wait time.

00:05:57.105 --> 00:06:02.401
It lists each agent, with Why not eligible and Routing detail, in words.

00:06:02.891 --> 00:06:06.990
The Dialer decision log shows the same for outbound work.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:13.041
Each decision lists the agents that were weighed, and why each was or was not eligible.

00:06:17.241 --> 00:06:22.170
Send complex calls to skilled agents, and let the requirement relax after a

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:24.301
wait so nobody waits forever.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:29.779
Give a priority line for key customers with the entry point's priority, and

00:06:29.779 --> 00:06:32.231
let aging keep everyone else moving.

00:06:32.981 --> 00:06:38.081
Spread work evenly with Round robin, or reward the agent who has waited

00:06:38.081 --> 00:06:40.201
longest with Longest idle.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:48.898
A call finds its agent through eligibility, skills and capacity, the strategy, then history.

00:06:48.898 --> 00:06:54.844
Priority, aging and overflow decide who goes first, and the reports tell you why.

00:06:54.844 --> 00:06:56.621
Thanks for watching.

